  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/chandramara-chandramara-2/",
      "html": "<p>Chandramara chandramara (originally Pimelodus chandramara) stays small, around 5-6 cm. It prefers to swim in open water and is diurnal. It is peaceful towards tankmates and disregards plants. It is a social animal, best kept in groups of 10-20 specimens.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/chandramara-chandramara-2/",
      "html": "<p>The species was described from the Ganges River in India by Francis Hamilton in 1822, and it remains virtually unknown from a scientific perspective.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/chandramara-chandramara-2/",
      "html": "<p>The water temperature can be between 16 and 26°C. It is favorable to offer different temperatures during the year, cool in winter, warm in summer. Any drinking water is suitable.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/chandramara-chandramara-2/",
      "html": "<p>Any common ornamental fish food will be accepted.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/chandramara-chandramara-2/",
      "html": "<p>Very droll and funny aquarium inhabitants that are completely peaceful against tankmates. Suitable company includes zebra danios (Danio rerio), rosy barbs (Pethia conchonius) and dwarf gourami (Colisa lalia), which all occur in the same habitat.</p>"
    }
  ],
